Here's how you can modify the `fetchWeather` function to handle city names with special characters or spaces:

### Updated JavaScript Implementation

```javascript
// app.js
const apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY_HERE';

async function fetchWeather(city) {
  try {
    // Encode the city name to handle special characters and spaces
    const encodedCity = encodeURIComponent(city);

    const response = await fetch(
      `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${encodedCity}&units=metric&appid=${apiKey}`
    );

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
    }

    const data = await response.json();

    // Validate the response data
    if (!data || !data.main || !data.weather) {
      throw new Error('Invalid response data');
    }

    // Extract relevant data
    const temperature = data.main.temp;
    const humidity = data.main.humidity;
    const conditionIconUrl = `http://openweathermap.org/img/wn/${data.weather[0].icon}@2x.png`;

    // Update the DOM with the weather data
    document.getElementById('temperature').textContent = `Temperature: ${temperature} &deg;C`;
    document.getElementById('humidity').textContent = `Humidity: ${humidity}%`;
    document.getElementById('condition-icon').src = conditionIconUrl;
    document.getElementById('condition-icon').alt = data.weather[0].description;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    document.getElementById('weather-data').innerHTML = '<p>Error fetching weather data.</p>';
  }
}

// Event listener to fetch weather when the button is clicked
document.getElementById('fetch-button').addEventListener('click', () => {
  const cityName = document.getElementById('city-input').value.trim();
  if (cityName) {
    fetchWeather(cityName);
  } else {
    alert('Please enter a city name.');
  }
});
```

### Explanation:
1. **Encoding City Name**: Use `encodeURIComponent(city)` to encode the city name. This ensures that special characters and spaces are properly handled in the URL.
2. **Fetching Weather Data**: The `fetch` function constructs the URL with the encoded city name, ensuring that the API call works correctly even with complex city names.
3. **Error Handling**: Proper error handling is included to manage HTTP errors and invalid response data.
4. **Updating DOM**: The weather data is extracted and displayed in the DOM elements.

By encoding the city name, you ensure that the API call works correctly regardless of whether the city name contains special characters or spaces. This makes your weather app more robust and user-friendly."
